    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's interesting. It's wild how much temperature affects them since they're so small. I've heard people say that sometimes a starter just seems to \"turn\" or go bad for no obvious reason even when you're following the schedule. Is there a specific point where the balance gets so messed up that the whole thing becomes unusable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When a starter \"turns\" or goes bad, it usually means the delicate balance has tipped too far, often due to an overgrowth of undesirable, less desirable microbes—or perhaps a sudden influx of contaminants. This is when the microbial population shifts from a productive, balanced community to one dominated by organisms that produce off-flavors or inhibit the good yeast and bacteria. It's less about a single \"point\" and more about a critical mass of imbalance, like when the mold spores or a rogue, aggressive bacterium takes over the entire feeding environment. If the acidity drops too low, or if the environment becomes too anaerobic or too contaminated, the starter can become too weak or even toxic to use in bread.",
      "source": "target",
